Le bloc K de sémantique du sous-langage K7PL ci-dessous est la source éditable normative
pour ce sous-langage expérimental. Le modèle K7OS séparé est explicitement non normatif :
il explore une hypothèse de profil sans l'imposer au langage. Le script Python est un outil
de vérification et n'est pas normatif. Les artefacts sont extraits dans
`src/k/.build/generated/` ; ces fichiers sont générés, ignorés par Git, et ne doivent pas
être modifiés directement.

La commande `lake exe spec --extract-code-only` effectue uniquement l'extraction, sans produire
le site. `make k-check` compile la sémantique du sous-langage et exécute ses exemples.
`make boundary-check` compile séparément le modèle K7OS et vérifie ses trois résultats.
L'extraction seule ne valide pas le code : les compilateurs et tests restent responsables
de vérifier les fichiers générés.

# Définition K expérimentale

```k7plK (file := "src/k/.build/generated/semantics.k")
 // SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Cyprien PIERRE
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: CECILL-2.1
 //
 // Experimental operational semantics for a deliberately small K7PL subset.
 // Normative source: spec/Spec/C5.lean and spec/Spec/C6.lean (repository-relative paths).

 module K7PL-SYNTAX
   imports INT
   imports BOOL
   imports K-EQUAL

   // K7PL Chapter 5 uses prefix S-expressions. Builtin Int and Bool values
   // are subsorts of K7Exp, avoiding duplicate syntax for literal tokens.
   syntax K7Exp ::= Int
                  | Bool

   // Only values may close the strictness contexts generated by K.
   syntax KResult ::= Int | Bool

   syntax K7Exp ::= "(" "+" K7Exp K7Exp ")" [symbol(k7Add), strict]
                  | "(" "<" K7Exp K7Exp ")" [symbol(k7Lt), strict]

   // Only the condition is strict: select does not evaluate its unchosen branch.
   syntax K7Exp ::= "(" "select" K7Exp K7Exp K7Exp ")" [symbol(k7Select), strict(1)]

   // Partial classifier for the two base types in this prototype.
   syntax K7Type ::= "IntType" [symbol(k7IntType)]
                   | "BoolType" [symbol(k7BoolType)]
                   | typeOf(K7Exp) [function]
 endmodule

 module K7PL
   imports K7PL-SYNTAX

   // One prefix expression runs in the K cell.
   configuration <k> $PGM:K7Exp </k>

   // Chapter 5 arithmetic example family. Rules use the source productions
   // themselves; k7Add/k7Lt are internal labels, not source syntax.
   rule (+ I:Int J:Int) => I +Int J [label(k7AddEval)]

   // Comparison returns a builtin Bool value injected into K7Exp.
   rule (< I:Int J:Int) => I <Int J [label(k7LessThanEval)]

   // Call-by-value conditional: strict(1) evaluates only the condition.
   rule (select true E1:K7Exp _E2:K7Exp) => E1 [label(k7SelectTrue)]
   rule (select false _E1:K7Exp E2:K7Exp) => E2 [label(k7SelectFalse)]

   // Partial classifier; ill-typed expressions have no typeOf result.
   // This is not the complete K7PL typing judgment.
   rule typeOf(_I:Int) => IntType
   rule typeOf(true) => BoolType
   rule typeOf(false) => BoolType

   rule typeOf((+ E1:K7Exp E2:K7Exp)) => IntType
     requires typeOf(E1) ==K IntType andBool typeOf(E2) ==K IntType

   rule typeOf((< E1:K7Exp E2:K7Exp)) => BoolType
     requires typeOf(E1) ==K IntType andBool typeOf(E2) ==K IntType

   rule typeOf((select C:K7Exp E1:K7Exp E2:K7Exp)) => typeOf(E1)
     requires typeOf(C) ==K BoolType andBool typeOf(E1) ==K typeOf(E2)
 endmodule
```


# Modèle d'exécution K7OS : frontière native (profil non normatif)

Ce bloc est un modèle d'exploration du système envisagé, pas une règle du langage K7PL.
Le booléen passé à nativeWrite représente uniquement l'hypothèse abstraite d'une frontière
appliquée. Il ne modélise ni une MMU, ni un IOMMU, ni une isolation matérielle particulière.
La définition reste éditée ici, puis extraite par Verso.

```k7osModel (file := "src/k/.build/generated/models/native-boundary.k")
 // SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Cyprien PIERRE
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: CECILL-2.1
 //
 // Non-normative K7OS profile model. It does not extend the K7PL language semantics.

 module K7OS-NATIVE-BOUNDARY-SYNTAX
   imports INT
   imports BOOL

   syntax BoundaryOp ::= "conformingCall" "(" Int ")" [symbol(conformingCall)]
                       | "nativeWrite" "(" Bool "," Int ")" [symbol(nativeWrite)]
                       | "boundaryResult" "(" Int "," Int ")" [symbol(boundaryResult)]
 endmodule

 module K7OS-NATIVE-BOUNDARY
   imports K7OS-NATIVE-BOUNDARY-SYNTAX

   configuration <os>
                   <k> $PGM:BoundaryOp </k>
                   <protected> 0 </protected>
                   <service> 0 </service>
                 </os>

   rule <k> conformingCall(I:Int) => boundaryResult(P:Int, I) </k>
        <protected> P:Int </protected>
        <service> _S:Int => I </service>
        [label(k7osConformingCall)]

   rule <k> nativeWrite(true, I:Int) => boundaryResult(P:Int, S:Int) </k>
        <protected> P:Int </protected>
        <service> S:Int </service>
        [label(k7osNativeWriteRejected)]

   rule <k> nativeWrite(false, I:Int) => boundaryResult(I, S:Int) </k>
        <protected> P:Int => I </protected>
        <service> S:Int </service>
        [label(k7osNativeWriteBypass)]
 endmodule
```

# Script Python de vérification des exemples

Ce script exécute krun sur les programmes maintenus sous tests/examples/ et vérifie leurs
résultats attendus. Il est lui aussi généré depuis Verso : il ne constitue pas une seconde source
à maintenir dans le dépôt.

```pythonScript (file := "src/k/.build/generated/check_k_examples.py")
#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Cyprien PIERRE
# SPDX-License-Identifier: CECILL-2.1
"""Run the experimental K7PL example corpus and check expected results."""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

EXPECTED_OUTPUTS = {
    "add.k7": "6",
    "select.k7": "3",
}


def main() -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--definition", required=True, type=Path)
    parser.add_argument("--examples", required=True, type=Path)
    parser.add_argument("--krun", default="krun")
    args = parser.parse_args()

    failures = 0
    for filename, expected in EXPECTED_OUTPUTS.items():
        program = args.examples / filename
        try:
            result = subprocess.run(
                [args.krun, "--definition", str(args.definition), str(program)],
                check=False,
                capture_output=True,
                text=True,
            )
        except OSError as error:
            print(f"Unable to execute {args.krun!r}: {error}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

        if result.stdout:
            print(result.stdout, end="" if result.stdout.endswith("\n") else "\n")
        if result.stderr:
            print(result.stderr, file=sys.stderr, end="" if result.stderr.endswith("\n") else "\n")

        if result.returncode != 0:
            print(
                f"{filename}: krun exited with status {result.returncode}",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            failures += 1
            continue

        if re.search(rf"(?<![0-9]){re.escape(expected)}(?![0-9])", result.stdout) is None:
            print(
                f"{filename}: expected output containing the integer {expected}",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            failures += 1

    return 1 if failures else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
```
